7. Animation and motion specification
The user explicitly wants animation included. Implement subtle premium motion that enhances product presentation without slowing shopping or reducing accessibility.
Recommended animation sequence for hero
Initial load:
1. Background/decorative shapes settle or fade in subtly.
2. Eyebrow and headline appear with a short opacity/vertical reveal.
3. Supporting copy and CTA follow with a small stagger.
4. Bike visual fades in and translates a few pixels into its resting position.
5. Specs panel fades/slides in gently.
6. After entrance, the composition settles. Avoid endless dramatic movement.
Provisional timing (tune after visual comparison):
- Overall intro: around 600–1000ms.
- Text stagger: 70–120ms.
- Small translation: 8–20px.
- Standard easing: a restrained ease-out/cubic-bezier curve.
- Interaction transitions: typically 150–250ms.
- Do not delay access to the CTA until a long animation finishes.
- Avoid large parallax, constant floating, fast spinning wheels, or distracting motion by default.
Scroll animations
- Reveal feature sections once as they enter view.
- Keep motion restrained and do not hide content from assistive technology.
- Avoid layout shifts during animation.
- Use Intersection Observer or an existing animation library appropriate to the stack.
- Do not animate every paragraph or component; preserve performance.
Interactive motion
- Buttons: subtle color/background transition and small press feedback.
- Product cards: restrained hover state; do not rely on hover for essential information.
- Color selection: clear selected-state change and image transition if variant assets exist.
- Accordion/cart drawer/mobile menu: smooth open/close with correct focus management.
- Carousel: controlled transition with working previous/next and accessible slide labels if a real carousel is needed.
Reduced motion and fallback
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: reduce.
- Reduced-motion mode should remove parallax, large transforms, staggered entrances, and nonessential continuous animation.
- The website must remain complete and readable with animations disabled.
- Use CSS transforms and opacity where possible; avoid animating layout-heavy properties.
- Avoid animation dependencies unless already present or justified by the project.
- Do not invent or depend on a video/3D asset that does not exist. If no verified 3D model is provided, use the real product image and subtle 2D motion. Document the limitation.
Optional 3D
Do not add WebGL/Three.js merely for novelty. Only use a verified 3D product asset and an acceptable performance budget. If there is no real model, use a static image and CSS motion. Never fabricate product geometry and pass it off as manufacturer-accurate.
8. Responsive design requirements
Test at minimum:
- Wide desktop: 1440px and above.
- Standard laptop: around 1280px.
- Tablet: 768–1024px.
- Mobile: 390px and 360px.
- Also verify a narrow viewport and zoom/reflow.
Requirements:
- No horizontal overflow.
- No text overlap or clipping.
- Product remains recognizable.
- Navigation works via touch and keyboard.
- CTA remains prominent and usable.
- Specs remain legible.
- Buttons/interactive targets meet accessibility expectations (aim for about 44×44 CSS px where practical).
- Decorative elements cannot cover text or controls.
- Image crops preserve bicycle wheels/handlebars/saddle.
- Breakpoints should be based on content and layout needs, not device names alone.
- Test at 200% zoom and verify content reflows appropriately.
9. Accessibility requirements
Target WCAG 2.2 AA where applicable:
- Semantic headings and landmarks.
- Correct focus order.
- Visible keyboard focus.
- Accessible names for icon-only controls.
- Meaningful alt text; decorative assets use empty alt text.
- Form fields have labels and understandable errors.
- Sufficient contrast; validate color combinations rather than relying on appearance.
- No information communicated by color alone.
- Motion preference support.
- Keyboard-accessible menus, dialogs, carousel, selectors, and accordions.
- Focus trapping and restoration for modal/drawer patterns.
- Use aria-live only where useful for cart/status announcements.
- Links navigate; buttons perform actions.
- No clickable div elements without appropriate semantics/keyboard behavior.
